 "drivingQuestion": "Why does R' Meir exempt these acquisitions from Jubilee return?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "bekhorot.52b.5",
   "daf": "52b",
   "sentenceId": 4,
   "he": "גְּמָ׳ מַאי טַעְמָא דְּרַבִּי מֵאִיר? מֶכֶר הוּא דְּאָמַר רַחֲמָנָא לֶיהְדַּר בַּיּוֹבֵל, מַתָּנָה וִירוּשָּׁה לָא. וְהָנֵי מִיְּרוּשָּׁה — יְרוּשָּׁה, דְּמַתָּנָה — מַתָּנָה. בְּכוֹר — ״לָתֶת לוֹ פִּי שְׁנַיִם״, מַתָּנָה קַרְיֵיהּ רַחֲמָנָא.",
   "en": "«GEMARA:» The Gemara asks: «What is the reason of Rabbi Meir,» who holds that none of these items return in the Jubilee Year? The Gemara answers: «It is» with regard to land acquired specifically by means of «a sale that the Merciful One states» that it «should be returned during the Jubilee» Year (see Leviticus 25:28), whereas «a gift and an inheritance» are «not» returned. «And these» cases listed in the mishna that are derived «from» the halakha of «an inheritance» have the status of «an inheritance, and those» that are derived «from a gift» have the status of «a gift.» The Gemara elaborates: With regard to «a firstborn,» it is stated: «“By giving him a double portion”» (Deuteronomy 21:17); here «the Merciful One calls» the portion of a firstborn «a gift,» an item that is given."
  }
 ],
